  <h2>The character-consistency problem</h2>
  <p>An uncensored model that never refuses is useless if it cannot hold a character. The most common failure modes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Voice drift:</strong> A stoic warrior suddenly speaks like a therapist after 15 exchanges.</li>
    <li><strong>Repetitive phrasing:</strong> Every response ends with "a testament to..." or "a shiver ran down their spine."</li>
    <li><strong>Passive protagonist:</strong> The model narrates the user's actions instead of advancing the scene.</li>
    <li><strong>Initiative without derailing:</strong> The model introduces new plot elements that contradict established facts.</li>
    <li><strong>Name and pronoun errors:</strong> The model swaps characters or misgenders a consistent persona.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>How we test character consistency</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>A detailed character card is loaded into the system prompt: name, appearance, personality traits, speech patterns, backstory, and relationship to the user's character.</li>
    <li>A 20-exchange roleplay scenario runs with fixed user inputs.</li>
    <li>Three human reviewers score each exchange on: voice adherence, dialogue naturalness, scene continuity, and absence of repetition.</li>
    <li>We also test whether the model follows explicit user pacing instructions (e.g., "let me drive the scene forward").</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>What the scores mean</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>20/20:</strong> Perfect character voice across all 20 exchanges. No drift, no repetition, appropriate initiative.</li>
    <li><strong>15–19:</strong> Strong consistency with minor repetition or one brief voice slip.</li>
    <li><strong>10–14:</strong> Noticeable repetition or voice drift in later exchanges.</li>
    <li><strong>5–9:</strong> Significant consistency failures; character breaks immersion repeatedly.</li>
    <li><strong>0–4:</strong> Unusable for sustained roleplay.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Models designed for character work</h2>
  <p>Purpose-built roleplay models (Aion 3.0, MiniMax M2-her, Cydonia 24B, Euryale 70B) are fine-tuned specifically for character adherence and dialogue. General-purpose models (Hermes 3 405B) can perform well but may require more careful prompting. Small models often struggle with voice consistency beyond 8–10 exchanges due to context-window attention decay.</p>
